※ pl4g-test: compile=error diag=4488

※ A wrap says one thing about every operator written inside it: that going past
※ the end of the type is what was meant and the low bits are the answer.  A
※ saturating operator says the opposite about itself.  Only one of the two can be
※ what the program meant, and which one is not something to guess at -- so it is
※ reported, and the saturating step that really was meant is written outside the
※ wrap with its answer handed in.

let a: u8 = 200u8
let b: u8 = 100u8

@[startup]
fn main() → u6:
    ⎕wrap(a ⊞ b)
